2 CONT -----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT June 2011: Bjarne Breilid of Madison, Wisconsin translated Norwegian text as follows:  “The Story of Torkild Aschehaug. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “In the book ‘Rakkestad Township 1814-1914’ by Torleiv H. Toftdal and Carl M. Krosby (printed by E. Sem, Fredrikshald {now Halden}, 1914), page 166: “PARISH MINISTERS, it states that Torkild Aschehoug, from 1792 to 1835, was born 
2 CONC on the farm Askehaug (or Holstad) in the Nordby Parish of Aas on August 17, 1757. His father was the farmer, Halvor Aschehoug (or Holstad). Through his own diligence and hunger for learning, he got so far that he could enter the C
2 CONC hristiania Latin School in 1773 and in 1775 he passed the artium examination. The following year he passed the second examination, and in 1797 he passed his theological tests and received his certificate. He then took a position i
2 CONC n Moss. In the year 1783, on December 3, he was appointed assistant chaplain for Rakkestad and ordained for that position on January 9, 1784 by bishop Schmidt. On November 23, 1792, he wasnamed parish minister for Rakkestad, where
2 CONC  he remained until his honorable retirement on April 13, 1836 with a pension of 400 Speciedaler, 200 of which were to come from a special education fund and it included the permission to use the property of the Fladestad widow as 
2 CONC residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT “In his application for dismissal from his position, he cites the following reasons:  “For about 18 years I have been almost completely blind in my right eye, and the left one is worn out. Speaking is very difficult since I have l
2 CONC ost all my teeth. My senses are very numbed because of a recently acquired paralysis in my right hand, making it so weak that I can hardly write. My legs are so arthritic that it is only with great difficulty they manage to carry 
2 CONC this old body which is leaning so close to the grave that I most certainly very soon will be settling my account with this life.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Most respectfully I petition Your Royal Majesty for Your gracious permission to be relieved of my position as Parish Minister for Rakkestad Township and most gracefully be allowed a pension.
2 CONT
2 CONT “It is with a heavy heart that I submit my most humble appeal since it has always been my wish to devote my last efforts to the office I always felt fortunate and lucky to lead, continuing until now. Now, in my 79th year, and noti
2 CONC cing the weight of the dark and difficult days of Old Age coming over me, I feel compelled to relinquish my position.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Among the reasons for which I hope Your Majesty will listen to my plea, I humbly cite the following: On the 3rd of December in the year 1783 I was appointed Assistant Chaplain here in Rakkestad, and on the 23rd of November, 1792 
2 CONC I was appointed Parish Minister. I am therefore perhaps the only one in Your Royal Majesty’s two kingdoms who has served as a minister continuously for more than 50 years in the same parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT “In the year 1790, deceased Bishop Schmidt wrote in his ministerial record, that ‘he was delighted to find religious knowledge widespread among the young people, and not the ignorance which was common among adults as well as among
2 CONC  the youth during the previous ministers’ rule. Deceased Bishop Bech wrote this in his ministerial book: ‘The parish minister, Pastor Aschehoug, has during the many years he has guided these congregations, had a beneficial influen
2 CONC ce on them: love of education, respect for religion, orderliness during public prayer, a sense for good and useful organization, and so many other good things - they were all the result of his enlightened and conscientious endeavo
2 CONC rs for the benefit and success of the congregations he was leading.’
2 CONT
2 CONT “My present Bishop (Sørenssen) has also during his visitations testified in writing to his complete satisfaction, finding everything in the most desirable order.
2 CONT
2 CONT “According to the testimony of the bishops, the many young people who were present, were very enlightened, and I dare say that these congregations are among the most honest and best informed in  Christian religion in the country -
2 CONC  to which a general library has contributed, a library I at my own expense established, which is free and has greatly increased the interest in reading.
2 CONT
2 CONT “When I accepted my calling to this position, assistance to the poor was in the most deplorable condition on account of burdensome daily begging. After discussing the situation with the community leaders and getting their approval
2 CONC , followed by considerable sacrifices on my part, I have personally made arrangement for providing for several poor people, including three of them in the parsonage, with the result that support of the poor has brought about a com
2 CONC plete elimination of begging during a period of 30 years. In addition I have on a yearly basis been provided with donations of sufficient food, enabling me to help someone who occasionally might be in need.
2 CONT
2 CONT “During the 18 years I served as pastor for the Mellemborg District, I never accepted pay for any service I performed as a pastor. Everything was free.
2 CONT
2 CONT “For 27 years I served as chairman of the Arbitration Board here in this district - so successfully that only rarely did a case have to go to court. I therefore had the satisfaction of receiving, through the County of Østfold, the
2 CONC  Federal Government’s recognition for the Arbitration Commission’s diligence and zeal. For about a dozen years the Commission held its meetings here in my own house - without having to pay rent.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Since the parish never had a qualified midwife, I learned through experience how important it is to have one, so that in 1819 I talked a brave, but poor girl to go to Christiania in order to learn the science of Midwifery. For he
2 CONC r stay and subsistence in the city, I paid without having been compensated or having requested any compensation except for the joy of having in her a capable and respected midwife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “For about 40 years I have been - and still am - mail clerk here in Rakkestad, having to deal with a lot of problems and even loss of money - for which I have had not one skilling in pay.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Through hard work and sacrifice of my own grain wares, money, and to some extent my own health I have been able to establish and direct a grain storage facility for lending here in the parish which now is the owner of about 7000 
2 CONC bushels of grain, with more than half of it in the storage facility.
2 CONT
2 CONT “In order to protect the congregation, none of my personal chaplains has been offered anything from the facility, but I pay them for the work they do.
2 CONT
2 CONT “During the time of those who came before me, the congregation’s farmers harvested the Parsonage. When I took over, I abolished that custom because I regarded it as being unfair.
2 CONT
2 CONT “I have never negotiated regarding payments for my clerical services, but allowed the person concerned to pay as he thought appropriate. I have never taken anyone to court or taken out a mortgage on someone’s property because of d
2 CONC ebt owed me although I am owed far more than 1000 Speciedaler, most of which I have little hope of getting reimbursed for.
2 CONT
2 CONT “My six sons, all of them scienfically educated, have been and to some extent still are royal officials. Two of them have died, unfortunately, as parish ministers, and have left me with 9 fatherless grandchildren, all of them in t
2 CONC he need of my support - as long as I have anything. Because of my sacrifices, especially during the war years 1808 and 1809, my king at that time graciously awarded me the Order of Dannebrog and equally graciously appointed me to 
2 CONC the Council of the Consistorial Court (Jan. 17, 1812). Your Royal Majesty, in 1821, at the close of the Storting {Norwegian Parliament}, were gradious enough to grant me the Order of the North Star and to give me the Royal Promise
2 CONC  of graciously taking care of me in my Old Age.
2 CONT
2 CONT “In addition to the pension your Royal Majesty will graciously grant me, an aging 79-year-old faithful government official, I beg you most humbly to give me the Widow’s property to my disposal. It is a small farm, assessed for tax
2 CONC  purposes at 1/2 skilling, until there is a minister’s widow in the parish. {Note from translator: I believe the meaning of this is that Thorkild is asking for permission to use a property set aside for widows of ministers so that
2 CONC  they would have a place to live in case they survived their husbands. If this is correct, there must not have been a widow using the property at that time.} My last most sincere and equally humble prayer is, that the pension I mo
2 CONC st graciously may be granted, may be paid to me from the State Treasury or from the Education Fund wince it is so difficult getting it through one’s successor.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Rakkestad, November 14, 1834. Thorkild Aschehoug.”
