2 CONT Intervju med Anto i Aftenposten 28 feb 1914:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://aftenposten.e-pages.dk/view/52900/50155/fullpdf/1.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1865, the year the American Civil War ended, Anton L. Westgard was born in Norway.  At the age of eighteen, he immigrated to the United States, looking for work and quite possibly craving a little adventure.  Census records fro
2 CONC m his early life indicate that he spent time in New York, Indiana, Illinois, and Pennsylvania, but by 1892 he was working as a draftsman and civil engineer in Texas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Travel appears to always have been a part of Westgard’s interests.  Before his thirtieth birthday, he had not only been all over the eastern half of the United States, but had also taken a couple more trans-Atlantic voyages.  On O
2 CONC ctober 19th, 1895, he married his first wife, Mathilde, in Chicago, Illinois.  Their three children, two sons and a daughter, were born in Providence, Rhode Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Westgard was on a map-making trip in the Philippines, Mathilde grew lonely in America and returned to her native Norway with the children.  Meanwhile, in 1903, Westgard obtained his first automobile, and his career took off.
2 CONC   After Mathilde’s death, Westgard married his second wife, Helen, and settled in New York with his children.  Helen and James, Westgard’s eldest son, sometimes accompanied him on his long pathfinding trips by automobile.  Even th
2 CONC e family dog, Pan, came along.  In 1911, Westgard achieved his first trans-continental drive.  He may not have been the first to do so, but he was the first to make the journey in a truck.
2 CONT
2 CONT As AAA’s pathfinder and field representative, Westgard continued to cross the country several times and wrote extensive advice for other motorists.  Among his many projects, he helped to plan the route for the Lincoln Highway.  Hi
2 CONC s work on the Park-to-Park Highway of 1920 was his last contribution to AAA and the Good Roads Movements.  While on the journey, he suffered from the last stages of syphilis and had to leave the touring party for treatment.  He di
2 CONC ed from the disease on April 3rd, 1921.  After seventeen years of automobile travel, he had completely worn out eighteen cars.  Westgard Pass in the White Mountains of California is named in his memory.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://depthoffieldproductions.pbworks.com/w/page/17514210/Westgard More Info]
