1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Peterzen, Conrad, ed.. Minnesota Naturalization Records Index, 1854-1957 [database on-line]. Provo, UT, USA: The Generations Network, Inc., 1999. Declaration of Intention 1906-1909, volume 1, p. 282
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew, Henry, Marie, and Bertha, and John Eide. Notes from Employment event: "Thorsnes began working with glass in 1916. In 1927 he opened the Glass Shop."
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes from Retirement event: "Operation of the Glass Shop has been taken over by Thorsnes' son Frank. Frank has been with his father since 1945. Retirement plans of Mr. and Mrs. Thorsnes include a leisurely trip across the US and 
2 CONC a flying trip to Norway."
2 CONT
2 CONT According to his daughter, Barbabra, Andrew Thorsnes came to America in December 1904 with his brother Anders Paulus, also known as Andrew Ness. They came to America on the ship Camusen through the port of Boston. Andrew went on t
2 CONC o California and was never heard from again. Marie Hansen and her sister Berthe were on the same ship but they did not meet at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a carpenter, and later opened a glass business in Astoria,Oregon which was later passed to his son Frank.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1930; Census Place: Astoria, Clatsop, Oregon; Roll 1941; Page: 2B; Enumeration District: 11; Image: 140.0.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1920;Census Place: Astoria Ward 2, Clatsop, Oregon; Roll 31109_4384835; Page: 4B; Enumeration District: 63; Image: 690.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1910; Census Place: Bemidji Ward 3, Beltrami, Minnesota; Roll T624_690; Page: 1B; Enumeration District: 272; Image: 133.
2 CONT
2 CONT World War I Draft Registration Cards, 1917-1918 Clatsop County, Oregon; Roll 1852053; Draft Board: 0.
2 CONT
